Ladybird no longer builds a use element’s shadow tree while adopting
it; it drops the stale tree, and builds the new one when the element is
next inserted into a document tree. Blink, WebKit and Gecko all defer
that rebuild too. Blink’s SVGUseElement::DidMoveToNewDocument() calls
UpdateTargetReference(), which reaches ScheduleShadowTreeRecreation().
WebKit’s SVGUseElement::invalidateShadowTree() hands the element
to the document’s addElementWithPendingUserAgentShadowTreeUpdate().
And Gecko’s SVGUseElement::TriggerReclone() calls the document’s
ScheduleSVGUseElementShadowTreeUpdate(). None of them builds the tree in
the middle of the adoption walk.
